</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     TIME DIFFERENCE & METHOD CARDS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section content-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
  <h2 id="method-heading">How to Convert ACST to EST</h2>
  <p class="method-intro">
    Because ACST uses a half-hour offset (UTC+9:30), converting to EST (UTC-5) involves subtracting 14 full hours and then subtracting an additional 30 minutes.
  </p>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 1</p>
      <h3>Two-Step Subtraction Rule</h3>
      <p>
        Subtract 14 hours from ACST, then subtract 30 minutes. Example: 11:30 PM ACST in Adelaide minus 14 hours = 9:30 AM, minus 30 minutes = 9:00 AM EST.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 2</p>
      <h3>Previous Day Calendar Rule</h3>
      <p>
        Any ACST timestamp prior to 2:30 PM ACST falls into the previous calendar day when converted to EST (e.g. 12:00 PM Noon ACST Tuesday = 9:30 PM EST Monday).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
