<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Mistakes When Converting ACST to EST</h2>
  
  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 1: Forgetting the 30-Minute Fractional Offset</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Subtracting 14 hours instead of 14 hours and 30 minutes is the most frequent calculation error, leading to 30-minute delays for international calls.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 2: Confusing South Australia with Northern Territory DST Rules</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      South Australia switches to ACDT (UTC+10:30) in summer, while Darwin and the Northern Territory remain permanently on ACST (UTC+9:30). Check the specific Australian city before converting!
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 3: Date Line Rollback Confusion</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Because EST is 14.5 hours behind ACST, any ACST timestamp prior to 2:30 PM ACST falls into the previous calendar day in Eastern Standard Time.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

